    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics has always felt a bit intimidating to me, even though I appreciate its usefulness. I did well in high school algebra and geometry, but struggled with calculus. It makes sense that I’m not the best at it, given my lack of formal math training beyond upper secondary. I enjoy physics because it's more intuitive and connects with real-world applications.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, I often get anxious about math, especially when dealing with abstract concepts. I tend to feel overwhelmed when I’m asked to solve problems that require multiple steps or rely on formulas I haven’t practiced. My anxiety isn't about failing, but about not knowing where to start. I often take a deep breath and try to break the problem into smaller parts.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I used an AI tutor a few times when I was trying to grasp basic calculus. It was helpful but not always accurate. Sometimes the explanations were too brief or skipped key steps. It didn’t replace my own effort, but it provided a good starting point. I mostly used it to check my work and to get a different explanation.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Start by writing the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / 2a. Plug in the values of a, b, and c. Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac). If it's positive, you'll have two real roots. If it's zero, one real root. If negative, two complex roots. Finally, simplify each value.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, take the derivative of the function, f'(x). Then, set f'(x) equal to zero and solve for x. Those x-values are the candidates for stationary points. Then, plug each x-value back into the original function to get the corresponding y-values. You can also check if they’re maxima, minima, or points of inflection using the second derivative test.",
